 @deandudek3921  SLI links the cards together so they can work on/with the same resources.  This is a necessity for games.

Production workloads (eg, video rendering) can be divided such that one frame doesn't rely on another, and, unlike gaming, you can render frames generally in any order.  In a multi-GPU scenario, there doesn't need to be any link between the GPUs to do the work.  For instance, you could send frames 1-50 to one GPU and frames 51-100 to the other GPU and not really have to care which one got finished first (or even what order they processed the frames, depending on key/i/b/etc. frames).

Long story short, not all workloads require that the GPUs be linked together (or even be the same type of GPU).

